@ 37.1.2 - Proto-Iroquoian to Proto-Northern Iroquoian
# citation: cedh audmanh, from Julian, Charles (2010), “A History of the Iroquoian Languages”, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg
	{ {, o,, o:[+long], }, {, u,, u:[+long], } } > { {, a,, a:[+long], }, {, o,, o:[+long], } }
	{ iji, iji:[+long] } > { hi, hi:[+long] } / :{ k_ “(possibly after all non-glottal obstruent͡s)”, s_ “(possibly after all non-glottal obstruent͡s)” }:
	i > e // _hCC ()
	∅ > i, i:[+long] / #_(C){ CC, C }VC(C){ C#, # }
	V > ”V / in antepenultimate syllables, if the vowel of the penultimate syllable was short */a/ followed by a single non-glottal consonant
	V > ”V / in penultimate syllables not preceded by an accented antepenult
	V:[+long] > V:[-long] // in #
	”V > ”V:[+long] / in open penultimate syllables followed by a non-glottal consonant
	h > ∅ / #_s
	n > ∅ / _ti
	t > ∅ / n_V